ably  stuff you've seen already so it won't  match up perfectly to what I saw  personally anyway this particular  chapter of the game starts off with  Ellie setting out on our own with the  goal to traverse the overgrown Seattle  streets in order to reach a hospital  these kinds of in-between travelling  sections will be familiar if you've  played the first loss of us they're  mostly linear sections of exploration  with minimal enemy encounters where you  can focus on searching the environment  scavenging for useful materials and  learning more about those who came  before you  in the last first part to this chapter  shows off an area that's got a lot more  to it than first appears you can spend  quite a bit of time exploring a number  of optional spaces if you've got a keen  eye these places often blend right in to  the rest of the city but are filled with  supplies  environmental puzzles and I mean  counters and side stories it might be  something as simple as searching the  employees only area in a convenience  store for crafting materials or  something a little more dangerous like a  ritzy diner filled with a bunch of  infected but also probably a bunch of  extra supplies I mean this is a place  you can certainly skip altogether if  you're not up for it  the solution here might involve steps  such as finding away outside the  building by breaking glass windows which  by the way is one of the most satisfying  actions to perform in this game and then  finding some rope and creating your own  way to swing into the room by our  another window an even more  time-consuming side area in this chapter  and revolved multi-story residential  block with its own subplot to uncover as  you go from apartment to apartment  culminating in something pretty  surprising which is something I'm  looking forward to talking to others  about when they experience it these  slightly more involved side sections are  usually more rewarding too you might  find a safe whose code you need to work  out by looking at environmental clues  you might find a place to upgrade your  guns something that's also really high  on the list of satisfying actions in  this game you'll find trading cards to  collect a thing that might prompt Ellie  to write a personal journey entry you  can read or books that unlock a whole  new skill tree fairly and getting the  sense that all this great stuff could so  easily be missed completely once you  move on really motivated me to pay extra  attention to the world and keep poking  around every corner I always felt like I  could be overlooking something and that  makes the world seem very full now Ellie  eventually finds herself swimming in the  sewers which is gross also you can swim  in the hospice part too and the chapter  starts the heat up by serving you a  bunch of large-scale combat encounters  you arrive at an overgrown city park  area and you're introduced to one of the  game's enemy factions often referred to  as scars they're the robed figures  introduced in the game's 2017 teaser  trailer and then later seen in the e3  2018 gameplay reveal trailer the group  behave like hunters methodically  sweeping the area in pairs  preferring silent weapons like bows and  melee objects and they communicate with  each other by whistling which can be  pretty unnerving getting hit by one of  their arrows will result in Ellie  continuing to lose health until she can  find the breathing room to remove it  which means taking on scars head-on can  be a little more damage  than usual now this park era you first  encounter them in is covered in tall  grass allowing you plenty of opportunity  to move around the area unseen and  perform guerrilla strikes from behind  before quickly escaping and re-entering  stealth the following area the entirety  of which can be seen to me  III 2018 gameplay reveal trailer I  mentioned they sees you encountering a  large group of scars in a more exposed  urban setting  it's a multi-story car park in a section