eings  I mean holy crap but the ruthless nature  of combat is also amplified dramatically  by the ways in which the enemies now  react and speak to one another something  that's more prominent with the  soldier-like faction that guards the  hospital  as opposed to the previous group of  scars plenty of videogame enemies have  Banta and box of course but when an  enemy in the last of his pots who  discovers the body of someone they know  and they say something to the effect of  oh god Alex no with genuine sounding  grief and anger I can't help but feel a  little guilty and when I shoot someone  in the torso but don't quite hit the  critical mark and they're like screaming  and like gurgling on the floor I can't  help beating myself up a little bit for  giving them a slow painful death it's a  lie  now I've seen a lot of people joke about  the presence of a PlayStation Vita in  the last of this part - these people  obviously don't understand the joys of  an oled screen nice clicky buttons and  like a lot of really great Japanese  games but anyway the more important  thing to note is the game the dead woman  was very obviously playing on it the  game was hotline Miami a game that  basically requires you to pull off long  strings of gruesomely creative kills in  a bloodthirsty rampage and mostly lets  you revel in it if you've never played  it hotline miami has vivid neon colors  and a thumping electronic soundtrack  that helps build a haze in which you can  kind of forget about everything except  doing the best murders it's hard not to  wonder about this weird Easter Egg and  start to think about the parallels in  comparison to the game at hand the loss  of his parts who also requires you to  pull off long strings of gruesome  creative kills but the difference here  is that the last of us feels like it  wants you to see those actions with  perfect clarity it's presented in a way  for you to feel the pain of every cut  blow and shot you give and receive and  the result 